{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, scale only history input embeddings by 1+0.01*log(1+c_i)/log(1+c_max), using fixed item counts from training interactions only. This mild frequency-confidence feature may reduce the relative influence of poorly supported context items; the previous rarity scaling regressed. Keep padding zero, candidate embeddings unchanged, and preserve embedding parameterization, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Save scales in each model checkpoint and synchronize predictor loading.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, shorten only the causal pooling half-life from three interactions to 2.5, retaining readout 0.8*h_t+0.2*m_t. The earlier improvement from four to three motivates testing slightly faster decay without increasing pooling strength, which regressed. Exclude padding and future states, and use the final real position at inference. Preserve truncation, features, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer; synchronize predictor metadata.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, replace dot-product compatibility with jointly trained diagonal bilinear compatibility: score(h,e)=sum(h*g*e), with one trainable gain vector g initialized to ones per category model. This starts at incumbent scores and may calibrate recurrent dimensions against unit-normalized candidate embeddings with negligible added computation. Preserve GRU recurrence, input features, embedding parameterization, sequence selection, readout, loss and optimizer. Update the predictor writer and checkpoint loading for the gain parameter.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, jointly set Adam learning rate to 9e-4 and weight decay to 1e-6, retaining width 128 and dropout 0.10. Slightly smaller updates paired with weak parameter shrinkage may stabilize fitting without the capacity reduction or reduced dropout used in regressed trials. Preserve features, embedding parameterization, GRU depth, history length, readout, scoring and loss. Retain seed 42, the 200-epoch cap, 200 batches per epoch, and one checkpoint per category."
}
